      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I getting caught up here.\n\nThere is a negative side effect of using texture compression.... If the atlas was done to minimize loss of pixel data without bloating, i observed unpleasant \"color banding\" when quality was not max for a tightly baked together 4k atlas were i made certain the exact size ratio of each  source texture was correct scale to fit on a 4k.  Here: Any texture size higher in my case would be wasted detail in the bake, and any texture size smaller would shrink a lossy texture at bake, and finally any compression setting would crush the colors uploaded and seen ingame on the avatar; all situations tested.  These are the points of minimizing original texture data loss and compression loss on disk only, to be clear.\n\nAs for unwrapping IO into the game, I don't use a 8k if i don't need to, but i would be very concerned  if any texture quality would not save on the blank space in a 8k image file and somehow at IO load, upwarp into a bloated raw image that is causing atypical avatar load times in the new loadin system. So the question is how is the texture data looking loading in the system memory and GPU?",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-08-30T21:40:12.093Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "With the newest updates to vrchat it seems to have fixed normal avatar hitches but introduced another.\n\nif a user has really high res textures 4k-8k (mostly 8k)\n\nthe game as a massive hitch when loading that avatar that dident happen before (guessing a cache or load issue)\n\nexample\n(i have a avatar with 2 8k textures \n(upload size is 6mb download)\nwhen i laod into the avatar for the FIRST time (when it dl's the avatar)\nit causes a massive lag hitch for everyone who loads it \n\nafter the inaital load it doesn't happen again (unless they have to re download the file again witch is another issue lol)\n\nthis did not happen 3 updates back with the exact same avatars",
